          {
            "typeName": "dsDescription",
            "multiple": true,
            "typeClass": "compound",
            "value": [
              {
                "dsDescriptionValue": {
                  "typeName": "dsDescriptionValue",
                  "multiple": false,
                  "typeClass": "primitive",
                  "value": "Do people share their feelings of guilt with others and, if so, what\nare the reasons for doing or not doing this? Even though social\nsharing of negative emotional experiences, such as regret, has\nbeen extensively studied, not much is known on the sharing of\nguilt. We report three studies on the sharing of guilt. In Study 1, we\nre-analyzed data about sharing guilt experiences posted on a\nsocial website called “Yahoo Answers,” and found that people\nshare intrapersonal as well as interpersonal guilt experiences with\nothers online. Study 2 found that the main motivations of sharing\nguilt (compared with the sharing of regret) were “venting”,\n“clarification and meaning”, and “gaining advice”. Study 3 found\nthat people were more likely to share experiences of interpersonal\nguilt and more likely to keep experiences of intrapersonal guilt to\nthemselves. Together, these studies contribute to the\nunderstanding of the social sharing of the emotion guilt."
                }
              }
            ]
          },
